WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.185
Can This Agent Do It—or May It Do It? A

2
00:00:03.185 --> 00:00:06.371
new generation of AI agents can inspect connected information, prepare

3
00:00:06.371 --> 00:00:09.556
documents, update a plan, draft a message, and keep working

4
00:00:09.556 --> 00:00:12.742
after you close the tab. That is a real change

5
00:00:12.742 --> 00:00:15.927
in what software can attempt. But it creates a question

6
00:00:15.927 --> 00:00:19.113
more important than the demo: Can this agent do the

7
00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:22.298
work—or may it do the work? Those are not the

8
00:00:22.298 --> 00:00:25.484
same thing. An agent may be technically capable of reading

9
00:00:25.484 --> 00:00:28.669
an inbox, preparing a proposal, updating a record, or drafting

10
00:00:28.669 --> 00:00:31.855
a post. That does not mean it has permission to

11
00:00:31.855 --> 00:00:35.040
see the information, authority to make the decision, or clearance

12
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.226
to take the action. The moment an agent is connected

13
00:00:38.226 --> 00:00:41.411
to real work, it can touch more than a task.

14
00:00:41.411 --> 00:00:44.597
It can touch trust, customer relationships, private information, money, commitments,

15
00:00:44.597 --> 00:00:47.782
and the record of what happened. The purpose of a

16
00:00:47.782 --> 00:00:50.967
control is not to make us afraid of new tools.

17
00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:54.153
It is to help us see where responsibility still belongs.

18
00:00:54.153 --> 00:00:57.338
Capability is not authority Vendor announcements can establish what a

19
00:00:57.338 --> 00:01:00.524
product is designed to do. They do not establish that

20
00:01:00.524 --> 00:01:03.709
the product is appropriate for every task, every organization, or

21
00:01:03.709 --> 00:01:06.895
every set of information. OpenAI’s current Dots documentation describes agents

22
00:01:06.895 --> 00:01:10.080
with cloud computers that can work with connected applications. It

23
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.266
says background research uses restricted read-only tools, while other actions

24
00:01:13.266 --> 00:01:16.451
are governed by permissions, rules, review, and approval paths. Meta’s

25
00:01:16.451 --> 00:01:19.637
Muse announcement describes an agent running in a dedicated virtual

26
00:01:19.637 --> 00:01:22.822
machine. Meta says people choose which applications it can access,

27
00:01:22.822 --> 00:01:26.008
can set an access level, and are asked before certain

28
00:01:26.008 --> 00:01:29.193
sensitive actions such as sending an email or making a

29
00:01:29.193 --> 00:01:32.379
purchase. Salesforce describes Salesforce in Claude as operating through the

30
00:01:32.379 --> 00:01:35.564
permissions and business rules an organization already uses, with organization-controlled

31
00:01:35.564 --> 00:01:38.750
checks for write actions and external communications. Those are meaningful

32
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:41.935
product conditions. They are not proof that an agent will

33
00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:45.120
be correct, that a process will be safe, or that

34
00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.306
a particular organization has made a wise connection. A product

35
00:01:48.306 --> 00:01:51.491
can be able to do something and still need a

36
00:01:51.491 --> 00:01:54.677
human decision before it is allowed to do it. The

37
00:01:54.677 --> 00:01:57.862
four questions before a connection 1. What is the bounded

38
00:01:57.862 --> 00:02:01.048
job? “Help with operations” is not a job. “Manage the

39
00:02:01.048 --> 00:02:04.233
business” is not a job. Both are invitations to ambiguity.

40
00:02:04.233 --> 00:02:07.419
A bounded job describes a clear input, a visible output,

41
00:02:07.419 --> 00:02:10.604
and a stopping point. Too broad “Handle our customer email.”

42
00:02:10.604 --> 00:02:13.790
Bounded instead Prepare a draft summary of three approved public

43
00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:16.975
support themes. Do not send anything. Too broad “Run our

44
00:02:16.975 --> 00:02:20.161
content.” Bounded instead Turn an approved transcript into a draft

45
00:02:20.161 --> 00:02:23.346
outline and three draft post options. Do not publish or

46
00:02:23.346 --> 00:02:26.532
schedule. Too broad “Fix our CRM.” Bounded instead Identify duplicate

47
00:02:26.532 --> 00:02:29.717
records in a non-production copy and flag them for a

48
00:02:29.717 --> 00:02:32.902
human reviewer. Do not change records. The smaller version may

49
00:02:32.902 --> 00:02:36.088
feel less dramatic. That is the point. A bounded job

50
00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:39.273
allows someone to inspect what happened, find errors, and decide

51
00:02:39.273 --> 00:02:42.459
whether the next step is appropriate. 2. What information is

52
00:02:42.459 --> 00:02:45.644
necessary? A connection is not a blank check. If an

53
00:02:45.644 --> 00:02:48.830
agent is preparing a draft from an approved public transcript,

54
00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.015
it does not need access to a full mailbox, a

55
00:02:52.015 --> 00:02:55.201
customer database, payment records, or private team conversations. If a

56
00:02:55.201 --> 00:02:58.386
task can be tested with synthetic, public, or expressly authorized

57
00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:01.572
non-sensitive information, start there. The National Institute of Standards and

58
00:03:01.572 --> 00:03:04.757
Technology describes its AI Risk Management Framework as voluntary guidance

59
00:03:04.757 --> 00:03:07.943
for incorporating trustworthiness considerations into the design, use, and evaluation

60
00:03:07.943 --> 00:03:11.128
of AI systems. In plain language: name the purpose, understand

61
00:03:11.128 --> 00:03:14.314
the risk, and do not hand a system more access

62
00:03:14.314 --> 00:03:17.499
than the work requires. What exact information does this task

63
00:03:17.499 --> 00:03:20.684
require? What information is useful but not necessary? Who has

64
00:03:20.684 --> 00:03:23.870
authority to grant access? What is retained, remembered, logged, or

65
00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:27.055
sent to another service? What happens to material already seen

66
00:03:27.055 --> 00:03:30.241
if the connection is later removed? The answer should be

67
00:03:30.241 --> 00:03:33.426
written down before the connection, not reconstructed after a problem.

68
00:03:33.426 --> 00:03:36.612
3. Which decisions stay human-owned? A human approval step is

69
00:03:36.612 --> 00:03:39.797
valuable only when the person can meaningfully review the work,

70
00:03:39.797 --> 00:03:42.983
say no, and understand the consequence of saying yes. Decisions

71
00:03:42.983 --> 00:03:46.168
that create a commitment, financial consequence, relationship consequence, or hard-to-reverse

72
00:03:46.168 --> 00:03:49.354
change should remain human-owned. Sending an external message; Accepting or

73
00:03:49.354 --> 00:03:52.539
changing a contract, price, or promise; Spending money or authorizing

74
00:03:52.539 --> 00:03:55.725
payment; Changing permissions, passwords, or account access; Changing customer records

75
00:03:55.725 --> 00:03:58.910
or contact preferences; and Publishing, scheduling, or promoting public content.

76
00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:02.096
A useful agent reduces the searching and assembling a person

77
00:04:02.096 --> 00:04:05.281
must do. It does not remove the responsibility to decide.

78
00:04:05.281 --> 00:04:08.467
4. What is the stop path? Every test needs a

79
00:04:08.467 --> 00:04:11.652
way to pause, review, and reverse course. Before an agent

80
00:04:11.652 --> 00:04:14.837
works in a live environment, identify the person who owns

81
00:04:14.837 --> 00:04:18.023
the task, the person who can pause or disconnect it,

82
00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:21.208
the activity record that will be reviewed, the actions requiring

83
00:04:21.208 --> 00:04:24.394
approval, the action that cannot be delegated, and the condition

84
00:04:24.394 --> 00:04:27.579
that ends the test. If no one can explain how

85
00:04:27.579 --> 00:04:30.765
the work can be paused, checked, or corrected, the system

86
00:04:30.765 --> 00:04:33.950
is not ready for a larger role. Approval is not

87
00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:37.136
the whole control system It is tempting to believe an

88
00:04:37.136 --> 00:04:40.321
approval button solves the problem. It does not solve every

89
00:04:40.321 --> 00:04:43.507
problem. An approval step may come too late if an

90
00:04:43.507 --> 00:04:46.692
agent has already gathered unnecessary information. It may be too

91
00:04:46.692 --> 00:04:49.878
shallow if the reviewer cannot see the sources used. It

92
00:04:49.878 --> 00:04:53.063
may be ineffective if the person approving is rushed, lacks

93
00:04:53.063 --> 00:04:56.249
context, or has no practical ability to reject the work.

94
00:04:56.249 --> 00:04:59.434
Purpose What is the agent allowed to accomplish? Scope Which

95
00:04:59.434 --> 00:05:02.619
systems and information may it use? Authority Which action can

96
00:05:02.619 --> 00:05:05.805
it take alone, and which must return to a named

97
00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.990
person? Evidence Can a reviewer see what shaped the output?

98
00:05:08.990 --> 00:05:12.176
Record and stop path Can the team reconstruct what happened,

99
00:05:12.176 --> 00:05:15.361
pause the work, correct it, or end it? This is

100
00:05:15.361 --> 00:05:18.547
not bureaucracy for its own sake. It is how a

101
00:05:18.547 --> 00:05:21.732
team preserves responsibility while using a more capable tool. A

102
00:05:21.732 --> 00:05:24.918
small test is not a weak test Choose one non-consequential

103
00:05:24.918 --> 00:05:28.103
job. Start with a draft, comparison, or research note—not an

104
00:05:28.103 --> 00:05:31.289
external action. Set the baseline. Describe today’s process and the

105
00:05:31.289 --> 00:05:34.474
quality criteria, reviewer effort, error types, and exceptions that matter.

106
00:05:34.474 --> 00:05:37.660
Use limited information. Use synthetic, public, or expressly authorized non-sensitive

107
00:05:37.660 --> 00:05:40.845
material whenever possible. Set the rules in writing. State what

108
00:05:40.845 --> 00:05:44.031
the agent may read, what it may draft, what it

109
00:05:44.031 --> 00:05:47.216
must not change, and what returns for review. Keep a

110
00:05:47.216 --> 00:05:50.401
record. Retain the source materials, outputs, review comments, and changes

111
00:05:50.401 --> 00:05:53.587
made because of the test. Decide from evidence. Keep the

112
00:05:53.587 --> 00:05:56.772
scope, revise it, pause it, or stop it. One good

113
00:05:56.772 --> 00:05:59.958
result is not a general claim about every task. This

114
00:05:59.958 --> 00:06:03.143
kind of pilot does not promise a saving, a staffing

115
00:06:03.143 --> 00:06:06.329
outcome, or a performance result. It gives a team something

116
00:06:06.329 --> 00:06:09.514
more durable: evidence about one clearly defined piece of work.

117
00:06:09.514 --> 00:06:12.700
Five sentences that should make you pause “Just connect everything.”

118
00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:15.885
More access is not the same as a better test.

119
00:06:15.885 --> 00:06:19.071
“It has approval, so it is safe.” Ask what it

120
00:06:19.071 --> 00:06:22.256
can read before approval, what is logged, and whether the

121
00:06:22.256 --> 00:06:25.442
reviewer has real authority. “The demo did it by itself.”

122
00:06:25.442 --> 00:06:28.627
A vendor scenario shows a possibility. It does not establish

123
00:06:28.627 --> 00:06:31.813
typical behavior in your environment. “It is only a draft.”

124
00:06:31.813 --> 00:06:34.998
Drafts can still expose information, shape a decision, or be

125
00:06:34.998 --> 00:06:38.184
mistaken for reviewed work. “We can fix it later.” An

126
00:06:38.184 --> 00:06:41.369
external message, changed permission, public post, payment, or damaged relationship

127
00:06:41.369 --> 00:06:44.554
may not be easily undone. The better question for builders

128
00:06:44.554 --> 00:06:47.740
The point is not to make every agent wait for

129
00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.925
an unnecessary click. The point is to place human judgment

130
00:06:50.925 --> 00:06:54.111
where it matters most. A carefully designed system can let

131
00:06:54.111 --> 00:06:57.296
an agent prepare a useful first pass while keeping authority

132
00:06:57.296 --> 00:07:00.482
with the people who bear the consequences. It can use

133
00:07:00.482 --> 00:07:03.667
a narrow connection instead of a broad one. It can

134
00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:06.853
distinguish an approved draft from a public statement. Before an

135
00:07:06.853 --> 00:07:10.038
agent enters a workflow, write one sentence that answers each

136
00:07:10.038 --> 00:07:13.224
question: What may it do? What may it see? What

137
00:07:13.224 --> 00:07:16.409
must it ask before doing? Who decides when it stops?

138
00:07:16.409 --> 00:07:19.595
If those answers are not clear, the next move is

139
00:07:19.595 --> 00:07:22.780
not a bigger integration. It is a smaller, better-defined test.

140
00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:25.966
The lighthouse is lit. Direction comes before delegation. Sources and

141
